zerkle@iris.ucdavis.edu (Dan Zerkle) writes:

>There are several monsters (blobs, eyes, rust monsters) that can have
>detrimental effects when you hit them.  Put in (perhaps as an option)
>a feature with a little "are you sure?"
> [...] It's really annoying to die because of one little slip.

Well, I agree that one should not be penalized for _typing_ errors,
but nethack is a game of discovery.  You have to learn how to
survive.

It would be completely against the spirit of the game to put a
confirm in when attacking 'dangerous' monsters, since that would
be a giveaway.  Next thing you know, we'll add a confirm if you try
to eat a kobold.

Perhaps a good improvement would be to allow the user to turn
confirmation on or off for each type of monster.  You know, like
OPTIONS="packorder:!&@#^&@#, confirm_attack=beR ... "

That way, you can arrange to use your knowledge of the bizarre world
of nethack to your advantage, without giving away anything to the
novice nethacker.  No defaults on this one!


>Other places that could use an "are you sure" are moving into water,
>stepping onto a trap that's already discovered.  Other possibles are
>quaffing/reading items already discovered to be harmful.

Okay... once again, the world of nethack is a dangerous place.
However, your nethack character should not be expected to know
how to spell, or type flawlessly to avoid death.

For entering water, I have suggested adding #swim.  Water should be
treated as a wall, insofar as normal moves will not enter it.
You can #swim in a direction to enter the water.  The command would
be listed in the manual, with no further explanation necessary.
The experienced player knows what pools are like, the novice does
not... but each character can choose what to do, and the player
is not penalized for typing goofs.

For traps, I think a confirm would be fine.  But perhaps this
requires more thought.  The character may not have the skill
required to purposefully trip or escape the trap.

Perhaps, for a discovered trap, the game could ask "avoid it? [yn]",
and your character could either try to trip it or try to avoid it.
Depending on the character's abilities, anything could happen.

About quaffing and reading "harmful" items.. I think the current
user interface gives you reasonable control.  I say, you drink
it, you suffer the next morning!  Besides, skilled players often
use these "harmful" items on purpose, in clever ways.  A confirm
would just get in the way.  Example: quaff blindness, smack a
floating eye!

>I agree with the identify business.  They way it's done could use
>improvement.

Yup... my character is certainly smart enough to browse the pack
and pick N items to identify.  It works for scrolls of charging,
it works in rogue ...  I wouldn't even mind dumbing it down a bit,
to where you couldn't go by categories.  If it worked like (d)rop,
that would be great.  Identify what? [a-fikl-o * ]

Well, I've blabbed enough for a while.  Thanks for your brains!

